course: dictionary
lesson: 6827
title: Meet the otter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An otter is an animal, so you can often learn a word's meaning from what the sentence says about it. Look for the clues: where it lives, how it moves, what it eats.
explain: «Real otters do not chase May-flies» sits in a story where moles and toads act like people. The word real tells you the writer is pointing at true otter behaviour, not pretend behaviour.
explain: In «a thick otter-like tail», the ending -like means similar to. So an otter-like tail is a tail that looks or works like an otter's.
explain: «young Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet» gives a clue too. Swimming is something otters do very well, so the sentence suggests otters are strong swimmers.
fact: An otter is a playful animal with smooth brown fur, short legs, webbed feet and a long tail.
fact: Otters live near rivers or the sea, and they swim very well.
fact: Their thick, waterproof fur and webbed feet help them move through water.
fact: Otters eat fish, crabs and shellfish.
fact: Sea otters float on their backs, and they can use stones to crack open shells.
exercise: What is an otter? | A bird that flies high over the sea | *A playful animal with smooth brown fur and webbed feet | A kind of small boat used on rivers | A tall tree that grows beside water
exercise: In «a thick otter-like tail», what does -like mean? | Not at all like | Hanging down loosely | *Similar to, like an otter | Made of otter fur
exercise: Which animal can use stones to crack open shells? | *A sea otter | A rabbit | A goldfish | A hedgehog
test: Where do otters usually live? | *Near rivers or the sea | Up in tall trees only | Deep underground in burrows | In a hot desert, far from any water
test: Which body part helps an otter swim? | *Webbed feet | Long ears | Smooth nose | Short legs
exam: In «Labradors were bred to fetch from water, with a thick waterproof coat and a thick otter-like tail», which word means similar to an otter? | *Otter-like | Thick | Fetch | Waterproof
